  "notes": "The Vallejo mobb core, and the research describes it by subtraction: sparse, 2- to 4-bar motifs, no busy fills, one synth lead plus one pad, fewer P-Funk interpolations than LA. So this is the lowest note density in the batch outside the two half-time entries — melody at 2–4 a bar, three-to-four-pitch motifs, percussion cut to a shaker and a rim, and the fill band already at its widest. The documented harmony is the mobb pair `i–VI–VII` / `i–iv–VII`, which is what this entry authors instead of the parent's seven families. ⚠ The two-layer bass — a deep car-tuned sub under a rubbery funk mid-bass — has no 808 lane to sit in, because the g-funk parent nulls `drums.bass808` for a genre with no 808 anywhere. It is modelled as ONE bass part spanning both layers: `register` [24,44] is 20 semitones and covers sub and mid, `followRootsProb` 0.82 carries the research's \"simple root/fifth motion,\" and `passingTones` is the fifth alone. The split itself is not representable and is named here rather than faked. ⚠ `modes` are authored because the parent's Death Row moods pin 90–100 and this band is 84–95. ⚠ `medium` is the research's own rating; the producers and the drum lineage are documented, the voicing detail is inferred from the mobb description.",
